     "Racial profiling means treating people as suspects because of race, religion or origin rather than evidence. In New York, the police stop-and-frisk programme led to Floyd v. City of New York. On 12 August 2013, Judge Shira Scheindlin ruled that the NYPD had violated the Fourth Amendment through unreasonable searches and the Fourteenth by stopping people in a racially discriminatory way. The two kinds of profiling share a weakness: both turn a statistical pattern into suspicion of one individual. India has its own long version of this, the colonial 'criminal tribes', covered later today.",
